<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Erie Nation</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1656 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Iroquoian people of the south shore of Lake Erie, which takes their name, destroyed by the Iroquois Confederacy in the 1650s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Erie lived in what is now western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>. Little is known of their early history; they appear in French sources mainly through <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/huron-confederacy">Huron</a> informants, who called them the Cat People, perhaps after the panther, and they are known almost entirely from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> Relations.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like other Iroquoian peoples they lived in palisaded villages of longhouses and grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash. They were said to be numerous and skilled with poisoned arrows, and to have had little direct contact with Europeans, so they lacked firearms. After 1649 refugees from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/huron-confederacy">Huron Confederacy</a> joined them, raising tensions with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seneca-nation">Seneca</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1654, after a quarrel in which <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seneca-nation">Seneca</a> envoys were killed, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy</a> invaded, and by 1656 the Erie had been defeated. Survivors were absorbed by the Seneca, and some may have moved south to become the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westo">Westo</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/erie-nation.jpg" alt="Erie Nation" width="320" height="200"><figcaption>Image: Self · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cat-Nation.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1656 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iroquoian traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Erie</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Erie, Eriehronon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1578&amp;focus=erie-nation" aria-label="Erie Nation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2270 775 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Erie Nation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3160 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2590 1031h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2590 1031h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United States. <a href="/atlas?year=1578&amp;focus=erie-nation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

